    {
      "role": "user",
      "content": "right, that makes sense. but looking at the gradient maps, it seems like the 'intuition' is pretty much baked into the core attention mechanisms across almost all the middle layers, not just a few isolated blocks. if we prune those, we're basically going to lose the ability to identify these specific phases entirely. is there any way to separate the mathematical logic of the findings from the specific weight configurations you used to find them? i need to know if stripping these layers will actually break the model's ability to verify the results.",
