    {
      "role": "user",
      "content": "the peer reviewers won't accept \"differential pruning\" or frozen layers as a way to show reproducibility—the standard is to use the base architecture without any of our specialized fine-tuning. they want to see if the fundamental physics holds up without these specific heuristic patterns. if i follow your suggestion and keep the weights intact, it's basically just a variation of the current model, which defeats the whole purpose of the verification. it seems like there's no way to prove the findings are \"model-agnostic\" if we can't strip the specialized layers. isn't it better to just document that the findings rely on these high-level patterns rather than trying to force a stripped-down version to work?",
